  "text": "**Solution 1 is wrong.** It assumes |s(S_n)| = C_{n-1}, which gives 2, 5, 14 for n = 3, 4, 5. Hand-checking n = 3 gives 2 (images 123 and 213), and n = 4 gives 5, so the Catalan formula fits there. But the image of s on S_5 has 17 elements, not 14, so the formula fails. The one-step count is not Catalan, so the claimed C_m is unsupported.\n\n**Solution 3 is wrong.** It assumes |s(S_n)| = B_{n-1}. That gives 15 for n = 5, but the image has 17 elements. Its conclusion that the difference is 0 is therefore unsupported. The problem also asks for a formula in m, which suggests a nonzero answer.\n\n**Solution 2 is unverified.** Its chain of values rests on c_6 = 68 and on a two-fold recursion P_N that it does not prove. I recall the sorted-permutation sequence as 1, 2, 5, 17, 70, which would make c_6 = 70, not 68, but I could not confirm this. Its final step fits a quadratic through three points, (4, 9), (5, 16), (6, 25). Any three points determine a quadratic, so this is weak evidence. The pattern (m-1)^2 may well be right, but I cannot confirm it here.\n\nNone of the three solutions is demonstrably correct. Solution 2 is the only one not refuted by a check I could make, but its key inputs are doubtful.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
